WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.000
- [Narrator] You've probably
asked the question before.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.140
iPhone or Android?

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:07.360
Apple Maps or Google Maps?

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.460
Safari or Chrome?

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:10.520
On the surface,

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.410
Apple and Google represent
the ultimate tech rivalry

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:16.150
but behind the scenes,

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:17.730
executives have maintained

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.930
a multi-billion dollar partnership

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:22.130
that benefits them both massively.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:24.020
- I would describe Apple and Google

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.187
as the classic example of frenemies.

00:00:26.187 --> 00:00:29.104
On the one hand, they're fierce rivals,

00:00:29.104 --> 00:00:32.620
on the other hand, they benefit greatly

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:35.330
from figuring out ways to work together.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:37.660
- [Narrator] Google pays
Apple an estimated 8

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.576
to $12 billion a year

00:00:39.576 --> 00:00:42.920
or 1/3 of Alphabet's annual profits

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.620
to make sure it's the
default search engine

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.100
on more than a billion Apple devices.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.670
That deal has helped Google
dominate the search market.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:55.250
In recent years, it's
accounted for 90 to 95%

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.600
of search engine queries in the US.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.250
The deal between the two tech giants

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.890
is so powerful that it's at the center

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.420
of one of the biggest
US government lawsuits

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:07.600
against a public company since the '90s.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.390
So how did two of Silicon
Valley's biggest rivals come

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:14.670
to form one of tech's most
valuable partnerships?

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.910
To understand Apple and
Google's frenemy relationship,

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:19.793
you have to go back to the beginning.

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:24.010
- In the early days,
they were quite close.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:27.810
At the time, the CEO of
Google was on Apple's board.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:32.168
It was part of the Silicon
Valley club, if you will.

00:01:32.168 --> 00:01:35.580
- [Narrator] In 2005, the two
companies laid the groundwork

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.970
for what would become one of
the industry's biggest deals.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.700
Google struck a deal with Apple

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.270
to become the default
search engine for Safari

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:44.980
on Mac computers.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:48.031
- As Apple evolved sort
of the relationship,

00:01:48.031 --> 00:01:51.131
in 2007 when the iPhone came out,

00:01:51.131 --> 00:01:56.131
Google was then the default
search for the iPhone,

00:01:56.210 --> 00:02:00.440
Safari browser and has really just grown

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.800
and grown from that point.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.800
- So Steve, I've had the
privilege of joining the board

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.990
and there's a lot of
relationships between the boards

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:14.120
and I thought if we just
sort of merged the companies,

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:15.553
we could call them Applegoo.

00:02:16.912 --> 00:02:20.500
- As it became clear that Google was going

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.494
to be more than just a search engine,

00:02:22.494 --> 00:02:23.845
that it wanted to grow

00:02:23.845 --> 00:02:26.644
and that Apple was going to be a rival,

00:02:26.644 --> 00:02:28.683
that became an issue.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.680
- [Narrator] In 2008, Google directly

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.340
challenged Apple's business

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.983
with the launch of its
Android operating system.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.550
The next year, Google
CEO Eric Schmidt resigned

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.010
from Apple's board.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.470
Since then, both companies have expanded

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:46.270
into each other's businesses

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:48.580
with Google launching Android phones

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.850
and Apple launching
services like the App Store

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:54.370
and Siri, which was originally powered

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.803
by Microsoft's Bing, not Google.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.563
- Search Wikipedia for Neil Armstrong.

00:03:01.749 --> 00:03:02.677
(beeping)

00:03:02.677 --> 00:03:05.110
- [Siri] Searching for Neil Armstrong.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.900
- [Narrator] It wasn't until 2017

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.000
that Apple switched from Bing to Google

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.890
for its search results on Siri

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:14.050
and Spotlight, the Mac search function.

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:16.970
That renewed deal between
Apple and Google came

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:19.380
at a good time for both companies.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.090
Google was facing competition

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.490
from Facebook's fast-growing
mobile ad revenue

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:27.770
and it's new deal with
Apple put its search results

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:31.450
and ads on more than a
billion Apple devices.

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:34.130
Now up to half of Google searches come

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:35.600
from Apple devices.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:37.450
- When you open up your iPhone,

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:40.810
and go to Safari and write something

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.330
that you wanna search for, boom,

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:43.500
you're going to Google.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.190
And that's a lot of value.

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:46.610
- [Narrator] For Apple, the deal

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:49.250
has benefited its business twofold.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:51.750
First, it got more
consistent search results

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.900
across Safari, Siri and Spotlight.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.677
But perhaps more importantly,

00:03:56.677 --> 00:04:00.550
the money it gets from
Google's ad revenue makes up 15

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:03.740
to 20% of Apple's annual profits.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:05.770
That's helped fund Apple's ambitions

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:07.700
to grow its services unit,

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.730
which has driven growth for the company

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:11.690
over the past few years.

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:14.010
- Apple is like a store.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:15.820
It's selling its shelf space,

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.280
that premo shelf space.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:20.780
And so when you go into a store

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:22.990
and you see those candy bars right there

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:25.480
at the register, that's the place to be

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:26.820
if you're selling candy bars.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.480
And so for Google, they're
in that default spot.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.220
- [Narrator] Now all of that money

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.410
and search traffic could be at risk

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.290
because of this lawsuit.

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:39.530
In October, the US Department
of Justice sued Google

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:41.744
over antitrust concerns.

00:04:41.744 --> 00:04:44.200
The government is alleging that Google

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.910
is a monopoly gatekeeper for the internet

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:49.550
and it says that harms customers,

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:53.090
advertisers and competing tech companies.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:54.740
One way the Justice Department says

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:56.360
Google maintains its dominance

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.230
is through exclusive business deals,

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:01.550
like through its partnership with Apple.

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:02.760
According to the lawsuit,

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.900
some people at Google called the prospect

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:08.910
of losing its default status
on Apple devices code red.

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:10.840
Google representatives
said they weren't aware

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.620
of the code red language
used in the lawsuit

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.545
and neither Apple or Google
have officially disclosed

00:05:16.545 --> 00:05:18.870
the exact value of the deal

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:21.320
or commented on the
Justice Department's eight

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.840
to $12 billion projection.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:25.270
In a recent interview,

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:28.130
the Journal's personal tech
columnist, Joanna Stern,

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:30.800
asked Google's former CEO about the deal.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:31.760
- There was ultimately a deal

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:33.410
that I did not personally negotiate

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:35.970
but it was a lucrative deal for Apple.

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:38.140
But important to say

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:39.707
that with respect to Google,

00:05:39.707 --> 00:05:43.010
and with respect to use of
Google search on Apple phones,

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:45.100
it is extremely easy to switch

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:46.720
to another provider.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:47.900
- [Narrator] Google has denied

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:49.850
the Justice Department's allegations

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:52.760
and said it plans to
challenge the lawsuit.

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:54.440
Its chief legal officer said

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:56.380
the lawsuit wouldn't help consumers

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:59.600
and that its relationship
with Apple is customary.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:02.757
Apple hasn't officially
commented on the lawsuit.

00:06:02.757 --> 00:06:06.980
So what happens if the Justice
Department is successful

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:08.060
in its case?

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:10.740
- This could drag out for many years.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.303
This is an interesting situation.

00:06:12.303 --> 00:06:15.806
In some ways, if the DoJ is successful,

00:06:15.806 --> 00:06:18.490
one of the companies that
might be harmed the most here

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:19.810
is Apple given the fact

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:23.350
that they perhaps had 15 or
20% of their profit coming

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:24.760
from this relationship.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:26.830
- [Narrator] Of course, there
are other search engines

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:30.890
out there, like Bing,
DuckDuckGo, Baidu and Yandex

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:33.060
but none of them even come close

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:34.800
to Google's dominance.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.310
Some financial analysts say

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:38.870
that if anyone can give
Google a run for its money,

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:41.800
it's Apple, and the antitrust lawsuit

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.530
might just be the push Apple needs

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:45.400
to divorce itself from Google.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:48.940
- We don't know exactly
where Apple is going

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:51.490
with its future plans here.

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:53.030
Some have speculated

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:55.930
that potentially this
would be a good reason

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:59.190
for Apple to develop
its own search engine.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:03.440
Others have said potentially
they might buy a search engine

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:05.530
to beef up that capability.

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:07.537
One of the things we know about Apple

00:07:07.537 --> 00:07:11.250
over the years is that it likes
to own its core competencies

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.594
so it could control those worlds.

00:07:13.594 --> 00:07:16.610
And so that's always kind of hanging

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:17.950
in the background.

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:19.100
- [Narrator] Apple hasn't responded

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:21.010
to requests for comment on plans

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:24.240
to create or purchase
its own search engine.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:27.500
Still, a breakup of this
multi-billion partnership

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:28.870
between Apple and Google

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:30.760
could deal a financial blow to two

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:33.130
of the world's biggest companies.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:35.140
And however this relationship plays out

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:37.040
in the future has the potential

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:40.230
to shape the way billions
of people use the internet.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:45.140
- [Tim] As we see Congress and
regulators debate the power

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:48.603
of tech, this lawsuit is
just yet another example

00:07:48.603 --> 00:07:51.380
of kind of that public debate

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:54.853
about their role in our
future and in our society.

00:07:56.225 --> 00:07:58.975
(dramatic music)

